narrative.Action:
Create four files, each named tsconfig.effect-diag.json and each sitting beside the project tsconfig it extends: at the repo root (extends ./tsconfig.json), in src/dashboard/server/, in packages/contracts/, and in packages/effect-acp/. Every one has exactly this body, with only the extends target differing:

{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "plugins": [
      {
        "name": "@effect/language-service",
        "includeSuggestionsInTsc": false,
        "ignoreEffectErrorsInTscExitCode": true,
        "ignoreEffectWarningsInTscExitCode": true
      }
    ]
  }
}

The three options are all load-bearing and none is optional. includeSuggestionsInTsc: false suppresses suggestion-category style rules such as unnecessaryEffectGen, which otherwise reach the output as 'message' lines and would pollute the seeded baseline; the issue lists style rule categories as an explicit non-goal. Both ignoreEffect*InTscExitCode flags keep the lane's exit code reflecting only real TypeScript errors, which the base gates already own.

This item is the isolation design: because extends resolves relative to the extending file, include and exclude are inherited unchanged, and because stock tsc only reads plugins from the tsconfig it was given, the real project tsconfigs cannot see plugin output. A probe confirmed this structurally — after patching node_modules/typescript, running the plugin-free root tsconfig produced byte-identical output to the pre-patch run. Do not add a plugins entry to any real project tsconfig.
